---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const coulombCfg = {
  curves: [{ expr: '0.00899/(x*x)', label: 'force between two 1 µC charges', color: '#1d4ed8', width: 2.2 }],
  x: { min: 0.01, max: 1, label: 'separation r (m)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 90, label: 'force F (N)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/electrostatics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Two kinds of charge exist — positive and negative — and like kinds repel while opposites
    attract, with a force obeying the same inverse-square law as gravity but about
    <strong>10³⁶ times stronger</strong>. Rub a balloon on your hair: electrons transfer, the
    balloon gains net negative charge, and Coulomb's law does the rest.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Coulomb's law for two microcoulomb charges — halve the distance, quadruple the force" widget="plotter" config={coulombCfg} />

  <p>
    F = kq₁q₂/r² with k = 8.99 × 10⁹ N·m²/C². Slide from 1 m to 10 cm (one tenth) and the force
    grows a hundredfold. That steepness is why static shocks are violent up close and nothing
    across the room.
  </p>

  <h2>Field, not spooky action</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Electric field E = F/q:</strong> the force a +1 C test charge would feel at each point. Charges fill space with fields; the field, not the other charge, is the local cause.</li>
    <li><strong>Field lines:</strong> exit +, enter −, never cross, and bunch where the field is strong. The diagram <em>is</em> the physics for conductors: inside a hollow conductor the field is zero (Faraday cage).</li>
    <li><strong>Potential V:</strong> field per unit charge, integrated over distance — measured in volts. Batteries, lightning and your nervous system all talk in potential differences.</li>
    <li><strong>Conductors vs insulators:</strong> conductors have mobile electrons (metals: one per atom loose); insulators hoard theirs. Semiconductors sit between — doped just so, they became civilisation's switch.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One scale to hold onto: in a hydrogen atom, the electric pull between proton and electron
    outweighs their gravitational pull by a factor of ~10³⁹. Electricity is not a small effect
    that got big — matter is held together by it.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Coulomb (1785); OpenStax, College Physics (Electric Charge and Electric Field).</p>
</TopicPage>
